{
  "name": "Schrödinger spiralling soliton pair",
  "description": "Saturable nonlinear Schrödinger equation: two in-phase solitons 30 cells apart, launched with opposite sideways momentum. Their attraction swings them round each other — about a third of a turn — as they spiral in and fuse into one breathing soliton. With less momentum they fuse straight away; with a little more (the lab found the edge at kx ≈ 0.023 in the packet's units) the pull only bends their paths and they escape. The bound orbit in between is a knife edge here, as for coherent light beams in photorefractive crystals (Shih, Segev & Salamo 1997), where lasting spirals need partial coherence or anisotropy. Things to try: the collision scene; the seed brush adds light a soliton can capture; s lower makes solitons tighter and their interactions shorter-range.",
